  "flatStackAngles": {
    "precompute-and-cache": "The query didn't need a live warehouse. Precompute the answer once, park it as a file, serve it from the edge for a rounding error.",
    "own-your-storage": "Their data was hostage to the engine that read it. Open formats on object storage means the engine is a choice, not a landlord.",
    "low-center-of-gravity": "A tower goes up fast and comes down in a strong wind. A flat stack survives a bad quarter because it costs nothing to keep standing.",
    "compute-on-purpose": "Compute should be summoned, not stationed. If it runs when nobody is asking, that is not infrastructure, it is a subscription to your own idle time.",
    "the-physical-bill": "Every abstraction eventually bills you in megawatts. The cheapest datacenter is the one your architecture never needed.",
    "fewer-doors": "Attack surface is a function of how many things are running. Static files on a CDN have no runtime to exploit, no dependency to poison, no credential to leak.",
    "static-does-not-fall-over": "You cannot cascade-fail a file. Precomputed artifacts on a CDN degrade to slightly stale instead of entirely gone.",
    "subtract-by-default": "The cloud made adding free and owning expensive. The discipline is deletion: every component you remove is one you never have to staff, patch, or explain."
  },
